      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fort Gaines was founded in 1814 as a frontier fort to protect settlers from Creek Indians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.georgiaencyclopedia.org/articles/counties-cities-neighborhoods/fort-gaines/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sits on a 130‑foot bluff overlooking the Chattahoochee River, which also marks the Alabama state line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.georgiaencyclopedia.org/articles/counties-cities-neighborhoods/fort-gaines/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Named for General Edmund Pendleton Gaines, the fort did not receive his troops until 1816.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Gaines%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1854, Fort Gaines became the seat of the newly formed Clay County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Gaines%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A restored blockhouse marks the site of the 1816 frontier stockade built by U.S. soldiers.",
